\tag{transient}
\title{GS Register}
\date{2026-07-09T20:56:02Z}

\p{
\strong{The problem:} the kernel is one code image running simultaneously on all cores, and it's full of state that must be per-core: the runqueue this CPU is scheduling from, the current task pointer, per-CPU counters and caches (slab per-CPU freelists, per-CPU refcounts), the per-CPU entry stacks, softirq state. Every core needs fast access to its own copy, and "fast" here means hot-path fast — current is read constantly.
  }

\p{\strong{The GS solution:} at boot, each core writes its own per-CPU area's base address into its own IA32_GS_BASE (MSRs are per-core, so each core's GS points somewhere different). All per-CPU variables live at fixed offsets within a per-CPU region, replicated once per core. Then the same instruction does the right thing on every core:
\pre\verb<<<|
mov rax, gs:[rq_offset]     ; each core gets ITS runqueue
<<<
}
